  Blog prompt finished in 5:23, 95 tool calls, $0.35
  Final report:
    The blog is built and running. The front page and both posts return 200 and render every block. I checked this with curl only, not in a browser.
    
    **What I built**
    
    - **Schema:** three block types (`richTextBlock`, `imageBlock`, `quoteBlock`) in the Blocks folder, and a Blog Block Grid data type that lists all three.
    - **Page types:** Blog Front Page (title, intro, body) and Blog Post (title, publish date, excerpt, listing image, body).
    - **Templates:** `blogFrontPage` and `blogPost`. Site Root allows Blog Front Page, which allows Blog Post.
    - **Media:** five Unsplash photos in a "Blog Images" folder.
    - **Content:**
      - "The MCP Blog" front page under WWW, with an intro and one rich text block.
      - "Why MCP makes working with Umbraco a joy" (1 Oct 2026): rich text, image, rich text, quote.
      - "Three reasons MCP is worth the fuss" (8 Oct 2026): the same four-block structure.
    - **Domain:** `/` on the front page.
    - **Views:** the two page views, three block adapters in `Views/Partials/blockgrid/Components/`, three ViewComponents with their views, and plain models in `Models/Blocks/`.
    
    **How to view it**
    
    Run Umbraco (it's currently running in the background) and open:
    - https://localhost:44317/
    - https://localhost:44317/why-mcp-makes-working-with-umbraco-a-joy/
    - https://localhost:44317/three-reasons-mcp-is-worth-the-fuss/
    
    Accept the self-signed certificate warning.
    
    **What I checked**
    
    - All three URLs return 200.
    - Every block's text is present.
    - There are no "Could not render" messages and no escaped `<p>` tags.
    - The front page lists both posts with the right dates and excerpts.
    - `site.css` contains the Tailwind classes the views use.
    - Both `dotnet build` runs succeeded with 0 errors.
    
    **Problems along the way**
    
    - **Publish cancelled:** `publish-page` with `includeDescendants` returned "Publish cancelled" twice. I published the front page with `save-and-publish`, then published the two posts individually.
    - **Build errors:** my first build failed on two things, both fixed:
      - `RichTextBlockModel` clashed with Umbraco's own type, so I renamed my plain models to `*ViewModel`.
      - `Model.Parent` doesn't exist on this Umbraco version, so the back link now uses `Ancestor<BlogFrontPage>()`.
    - **Stray file:** one of my curl commands wrote a response body to `/tmp/x`, against your rules. I deleted it straight away. Nothing else was written outside the project.
    
    **Not checked**
    
    - I didn't look at the pages in a browser, so the styling is untested visually.
    - I didn't check whether the uSync export picked up the new schema.

